  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{11}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ a & 2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
